<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese Constituent Cortes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portuguese Constituent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> was the elected assembly that met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> from January 1821 to November 1822 after the liberal revolution of 1820 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>. It drew up Portugal's first constitution, adopted in 1822, and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> VI back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Its attempts to restore Lisbon's authority over Brazil and to recall Prince Pedro provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grito-do-ipiranga">Brazilian independence</a> in September 1822.</p></div>
